<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1930.gif><a name=1930> 1930: Uruguay</a></h1>

The first World Cup had thirteen participants:
	<ul>
	<li>Argentina, Bolivia, Brazil, Chile, Paraguay, Peru and the host country South
	America.
	<li> United States and Mexico from North and Central America.
	<li> Belgium, Romania, Yugoslavia, and France from Europe.
	</ul>
In the Championship Game, Uruguay vs. Argentina, Uruguay scored first through Pablo Dorado.
Argentina's Carlos Peucelle tied the game eight minutes later and took the lead when striker
Guillermo Stabile scored.
<p>
Pedro Cea tied the game for Uruguay early in the second half.  Goals by Santos Iriarte and
Hector Castro guaranteed the win for Uruaguy.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1934.gif><a name=1934>1934: Italy</a></h1>

The use of qualifying rounds helped to being the initial entry of 32 countries down to the
16 that would play in a "Lose one, You're Out" format for the World Cup.
<p>
The Championship Game between Czechoslavkia and Italy went scoreless for the first
70 minutes when Antonin Puc scored for the Czech team.  With 10 minutes left to play,
Raimundo Orsi scored and sent the game into overtime.  Five minutes into the extra
period, Angelo Schiavio scored the winning goal for Italy.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1938.gif><a name=1938>1938: France</a></h1>

The 1939 World Cup started with thirty-six countries entered, only sixteen making it to
the Final Stages.  The Germans, with four top Austrians, were favored to win, however,
Switzerland shocked the Germans in the first round, coming back while down two goals to 
win 4-2.
<p>
In the Championship Game, Hungary meet Italy, who took a 3-1 half time lead.  Italy
continued their dominance of the game winning 4-2, with two goals from their striker,
Silvio Piola, and their left winger, Gino Colaussi.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1950.gif><a name=1950>1950: Brazil</a></h1>

Following World War II, the World Cup resumed in Brazil, far from the devastation
and turmoil.  The British countries re-joined the games, after having boycotted previous
World Cups.
<p>
The format of the tournament included two round-robin series, with Sweden, Spain, Brazil,
and Uruguay, progressing to the final four.  In the Championship Game, Brazil and Uruguay
staged one fo the most exciting finals every played.  In front of over 200,000 fans at
Maracana Stadium in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil scored first.  Uruguay, led by their captain
Obdulio Varela surged back to score twice, the final score coming from Alcides Ghiggia in the
79th minute.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1954.gif><a name=1954>1954: Switzerland</a></h1>

Hungary lost to West Germany in the Championship Game. even though Hungary had beaten 
the same team earlier in the year.  In that first game, Germany benched several top
players but in the final fielded its top squad to win 3-2.
<p>
An injured Ferenc Puskas scored first for Hungary after six minutes of the final, and
Zoltan Azibor added another two minutes later.  West Germany came back and with six
minutes from time, Helmut Rahn scored the winning goal.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1958.gif><a name=1958>1958: Sweden</a></h1>

The World Cup in Sweden saw the arrival of Brazil at the peak of the soccer world,
an event that has a lot to do with the emergence of 17-year-old superstar named
Edson Arantes do Nascimento, better know as Pele.
<p>
Brazil swept through group play, beat the Welsh in the quarter-finals, and pummelled France,
5-2, in the semi-finals.
<p>
Brazil beat Sweden by 5-2 inthe Championship Game, with goals coming from Pele (2),
Vava (2), and Zagalo.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>


<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1962.gif><a name=1962>1962: Chile</a></h1>

A record 52 countries tried to qualify for the 1962 World Cup, with 15 making it to
Chile.
<p>
Garrincha of Brazil shown brightly during the games, was the star in the 3-1 quarter-final 
victory over England by scoring two goals then getting another two in a 4-2 semi-final win
over Chile.  
<p>
In the final, Czechoslovakia opened the scoring but failed to stop Amarildo, Zito, and Vava,
who accounted for Brazil's goals in the 3-1 victory.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1966.gif><a name=1966>1966: England</a></h1>

England, the originators of the modern game, hosted the 1966 World Cup Games.
<p>
The host met West Germany in the Championship Game, and held a 2-1
lead with sceonds to go but Germany tied.  England rallied in overtime with a controversial
goal from Geoff Hurst.  Then, in the last seconds, Hurst ran the length of the field to score
his third goal.  Final score: 4-2.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1970.gif><a name=1970>1970: Mexico</a></h1>

Some see the Brazilian team that beat Italy 4-1 in the Championship Games as the
best side ever.  But the best game of was West Germay's 4-3 semi-final win
over Italy.
<p>
First West Germany tied the score at 1-1 with seconds to go.  In overtime, Germany
went ahead after only five minutes, but Italy struck back twice.  Germany tied the
score again before Gianni Rivera finally won it for Italy.
<P>
It is doubtful that anyh team could have stopped Brazil, whose third World Cup 
Championship earned them the right to keep the Jules Rimet Trophy for good.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1974.gif><a name=1974>1974: W. Germany</a></h1>

This was the hear that the Netherlands, led by Johan Crufyy, introductd "Total Soccer"-
a revolving, flexible formation in which players had definite roles but played different
positions as the situations arose.
<p>
Against West Germany in the Championship Game, the Netherlands took a 1-0 lead in
the first minute.  Cruyff was brought down in the penalty area and Johan Neeskens
scored from the spot.
<p>
West Germany, led by Franz Beckenbauer, fought back to tie in the 25th minute.
<p>
The winning goal came two minutes before half-time, when Gerd Buller
turned on a ball in the penalty area and fired a low shot in the net.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1978.gif><a name=1978>1978: Argentina</h1>

The Netherlands was without out Johan Cruyff but managed to make it to the
Championship Game, this time against Argentina.
<p>
The game was a scrappy affair with many stoppages. Mario Kempes scored first for Argentina,
but the Netherlands tied by a header from Dick Nanninga.
<p>
In the 14th minute of overtime, Kempes again put Argentina ahead, beating three men en route
to the goal.  With five minutes left, Daniel Bertoni scored Argentina's third goal.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>


<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1982.gif><a name=1992>1982: Spain</a></h1>

This was the first World cup finals to accomodate 24 teams.  In the Championship game, Italy
meet West Germany.  Italy, who had eliminated Brazil 3-0 and Poland 2-0, and West Germany, who
reached the final via a penalty kick shootout in its semi-final with France.
<p>
After a scoreless first half, Italy began to dominate and went up 3-0.  West Germany got one
back, but the margin was too great to close.  Italy won its third World Championship.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1986.gif><a name=1986>1986: Mexico</a></h1>

After Colombia pulled out as the host, the tournament was awarded to Mexico, which became the
first country to hold two World Cups.
<p>
Brazil was the early favorite, but Argentina grew stronger as the tournament went on,
eliminating England 2-1 in the quarter-finals, and Belgium by 2-0 in the semis.  In the
Championshiop Game, Argentina ran up against West Germany, which was appearing in its
third such game in four tournaments.
<p>
Guided by Diego Maradona, Argentina ran up a 2-0 lead, but goals in the 73rd and 81st minute
tied things up and the game looked to have swung in the German's favor.
<p>
Two mintes later, Maradona set Jorge Burruchaga free to score the winning goal.
<p>
<a href=#TOC>[<tt>Return to TOC</tt>]</a>
<p>

<hr>
<h1><img align=middle src=/wc94/images/posters/poster.1990.gif><a name=1990>1990: Italy</a></h1>

Once again Argentina and West Germany met in the final, only this time the Germans won 1-0 in
a Championship game sullied by Argentinians Pedro Monzon and Gustavo Dezottti
being sent off for rough play.
<p>
It was not a good World Cup for Argentina.  Its team leader Diego Maradona was a shadow of the
magician he'd been in 1986, and team play was defensive from the start.  Infact, Argentina won
only two games in regulation time, winning its quarter-final against Yugoslavia and its
semi-final against England in penalty kick shootouts.  It even lost its opening game
1-0 to Cameroon.
<p>
Cameroon, it turned out, was the hit of the World Cup, reaching the quarter-finals only
to lose to Englnd in overtime.

<p>
